{"type":"text","timestamp":1790897283007,"sessionID":"ses_f06353c07ffegrxTCvX2CYLafu","part":{"id":"prt_0f9cbd748001AzLCyAq57PC5jz","messageID":"msg_0f9cbc3000019yw1DP7fKY8S74","sessionID":"ses_f06353c07ffegrxTCvX2CYLafu","type":"text","text":"Answer submitted. Summary of the findings:\n\n- **signing_key_position = last**: The newest (last) key in the list is used for signing — `derive_key()` defaults to `self.secret_keys[-1]`.\n- **primary_verification_key_order = newest-first**: `Signer.verify_signature` iterates `reversed(self.secret_keys)`.\n- **fallback_key_order = oldest-first**: `Serializer.iter_unsigners` passes `self.secret_keys` in original (oldest→newest) order to each fallback signer.\n- **primary_before_fallback = true**: `iter_unsigners` yields the primary signer first, then the fallback signers, and `loads` tries them in that order.\n\nNote the asymmetry: within the primary `Signer`, keys are checked newest-first (`reversed`), while fallback signers are constructed with each key oldest-first — so the primary signer tries the newest key on its first pass, but fallbacks start from the oldest key.","time":{"start":1790897280840,"end":1790897282998}}}
